              ],
              "start": 0,
              "end": 142,
              "quote": "会议是让自己被人注意到的重要途径。当你在会上“表现不俗”时，你就表明了自己很自信，这能让你脱颖而出，被看作未来的领导者。遗憾的是，同事们读不懂你的心思。所以，无论你脑子里有多少好点子，在你把它们说出来之前，它们对你、你的团队和你的单位都毫无用处。下面就是让你在会上说出自己声音的指南。"
            },
            {
              "paragraphId": "tr-p2",
              "sourceParagraphIds": [
                "block:paper-mock-jiangsu-subei-eight-cities-2025-05:seven-choices:2"
              ],
              "start": 143,
              "end": 252,
              "quote": "有备而来。表达想法之前，先计划好几个要点。和一位信得过的同事练一练，他能帮你理顺各种问题。设想一下别人对你的想法可能会有哪些反应。尽可能从更多别人的角度去审视它。针对你的想法，可能会冒出哪些问题？你会怎样回答这些问题？"
            },
            {
              "paragraphId": "tr-p3",
              "sourceParagraphIds": [
                "block:paper-mock-jiangsu-subei-eight-cities-2025-05:seven-choices:3"
              ],
              "start": 253,
              "end": 361,
              "quote": "直奔主题。先用想法的要点吸引大家，再深入细节。开头有力，就给了他们继续听下去的理由。时间是宝贵的。人们在工作中被无数待办的事情压得喘不过气。“注意力只有八秒”或许只是个传说，但如今人们无疑比以往任何时候都更看重简短。"
            },
            {
              "paragraphId": "tr-p4",
              "sourceParagraphIds": [
                "block:paper-mock-jiangsu-subei-eight-cities-2025-05:seven-choices:4"
              ],
              "start": 362,
              "end": 465,
              "quote": "把它变成一场对话。人们听冗长的讲话时会失去兴趣。邀请他们参与到你的想法中来。组织论证时，要让他们想提问、想了解更多。给他们时间提问，他们就会更感兴趣、更专注、更投入。你越能调动他们参与，他们就越会真正倾听。"
            },
            {
              "paragraphId": "tr-p5",
              "sourceParagraphIds": [
                "block:paper-mock-jiangsu-subei-eight-cities-2025-05:seven-choices:5"
              ],
              "start": 466,
              "end": 592,
              "quote": "对自己有信心。你越相信自己，表达想法时这份自信就越会流露出来。你说话时，别人能从你的声音里听出自信，从你的肢体语言里看出自信。如果你任由自己觉得紧张，你就会让自己真的紧张起来。花一点时间摆一个力量姿势来对抗紧张。这能给你时间让头脑安静下来、集中注意力。"
            }
          ]
        },
        "completedSourceTexts": [
          {
            "textId": "done-seven-p1",
            "paragraphId": "block:paper-mock-jiangsu-subei-eight-cities-2025-05:seven-choices:1",
            "text": "Meetings are a key way to get yourself noticed. When you “hold your own” in a meeting, you show that you’re confident, and this can mark you out as a future leader. Unluckily, your colleagues can’t read minds. So no matter how many great ideas you have in your head, they’re useless to you, your team, and your organization until you express them. Here is the guide to getting your voice heard at meetings."
          },
          {
            "textId": "done-seven-p2",
            "paragraphId": "block:paper-mock-jiangsu-subei-eight-cities-2025-05:seven-choices:2",
            "text": "Come prepared. Have some talking points planned out before you express your idea. Practice with a trusted colleague who can help you iron out any problems. Imagine the possible reactions people might have to your idea. Look at it from as many other perspectives as possible. What are some questions that might come up in response to your idea? How will you answer those questions?"
          },
          {
            "textId": "done-seven-p3",
            "paragraphId": "block:paper-mock-jiangsu-subei-eight-cities-2025-05:seven-choices:3",
            "text": "Get to the point. Engage people with the main points of the idea, then dive into the details. When you start strong you’ll give them a reason to continue listening. Time is precious. People are overwhelmed with numerous things to do at work. While the eight second attention span may be a myth, people certainly appreciate brevity (简短) now more than ever."
          },
          {
            "textId": "done-seven-p4",
            "paragraphId": "block:paper-mock-jiangsu-subei-eight-cities-2025-05:seven-choices:4",
            "text": "Make it a conversation. People lose interest when they’re listening to a long talk. Invite them to be a part of your idea. Organize your argument in a way that makes them want to ask questions and learn more. When you give them time to ask questions, they’ll be more interested, attentive, and engaged. The more you can engage them, the more they’ll truly listen."
          },
          {
            "textId": "done-seven-p5",
            "paragraphId": "block:paper-mock-jiangsu-subei-eight-cities-2025-05:seven-choices:5",
            "text": "Have confidence in yourself. The more you believe yourself, the more that shows through when you express an idea. People can hear the confidence in your voice when you speak and see it in your body language. If you allow yourself to think that you’re nervous, you’ll convince yourself to be. Fight nerves by taking a few moments to stand in a power pose. It gives you time to quiet your mind and focus."
          }
        ],
